--- body ---

## Context

Found by the verification seat on PR #533 (ticket 0779), at round 1, and
recorded there as a non-blocking residual rather than a bounce:

> The one gap I could reproduce: a *custom* `SITTER_FORGE_COMMAND` that exits 0
> and prints nothing is trusted as "zero open PRs" — I built exactly this stub
> and ran it; the guard printed "OK (rule 3): 0 open pull-request head(s) read"
> and exited 0. This is a real instance of the trap this ticket exists to
> close, but it lives at the override boundary the harness-extension-point
> deliberately delegates to another workshop's own command, not in the shipped
> default (whose own docstring's guarantee — "empty only after a 200 that parsed
> as a JSON array of numbered objects" — I verified holds for
> `forge_open_prs.py` specifically).
>
> — <https://github.com/MinhHaDuong/search-works-for-zotero/pull/533#issuecomment-5645195629>

The reproduction is the seat's own stub, not a reading of the code. That
matters: 0779's whole premise is that a check whose all-clear cannot be told
from "I could not look" is not a check, and this is that conflation, sitting
one layer out from where 0779 fixed it.

**Be precise about which guarantee holds and which does not.** 0779 put the
remedy in the protocol rather than in the caller's discipline: every form of
`bench/forge_open_prs.py`'s protocol answers about at most one pull request, so
no caller can express the one-shot list-plus-filter, and an empty enumeration is
printed only after a 200 whose body parsed as a JSON array of objects each
carrying an integer `number`. The seat verified that guarantee holds **for the
shipped default**. What it does not cover is the substitute: `forge()` is marked
`# harness-extension-point` precisely so another workshop can drop in its own
executable, and the guard reads that executable's exit 0 plus empty stdout as
"this forge has no open pull requests." A stub that never looked, a command that
is a no-op on a forge it does not understand, a wrapper whose credentials
expired quietly, and a genuinely empty repository are one output today.

So the question this ticket exists to answer is not "how do we validate GitHub
JSON" — that is done — but: **what must a replaceable command prove about
itself before its silence may be read as an empty enumeration?** The boundary
is a contract between the guard and an executable nobody in this repository
wrote, and a contract whose compliant and non-compliant answers are identical is
not one.

Worth keeping in view while answering: the override is also how 0779's own test
suite stays offline and how its positive control was driven against real forge
data, so whatever is required must be cheap enough that a stand-in in a test
fixture can satisfy it honestly. A requirement that makes the test stubs lie is
a worse outcome than the gap.

## Relevant files

- `bench/check_sitter_version.py` — `forge()` (the `# harness-extension-point`),
  `answered()`, `open_pull_requests()`, and rule 3's `check_open_branches()`;
  also the module docstring's account of the protocol's shape and of the
  NOT-RUN discipline, which is where any new requirement has to be written down
- `bench/forge_open_prs.py` — the shipped default, and the reference for what a
  compliant command does; its docstring owns the protocol
- `tests/test_check_sitter_version.py` — `forge_stub()` and `broken_forge()`,
  the stand-ins every rule-3 fixture runs through, and the arms that pin the
  empty/malformed distinction for the default reader
- `tickets/0779-the-sitter-version-guard-cannot-see-a-ve.erg` — the parent, and
  its record of why the protocol rather than the caller carries the fix
- `tickets/AGENTS.md` — the enumeration trap in its original ticket-ID form, and
  the `# harness-extension-point` convention

## Actions

1. Decide what a command must do to distinguish "I enumerated, there are none"
   from "I did not look", and write the decision into the protocol's owning
   docstring so a future implementer meets it before writing an executable.
   Options visible from here, none pre-chosen and none exclusive:
   - **a distinct exit code for an empty-but-real enumeration** (as
     `FORGE_ABSENT` = 3 already distinguishes an absent path from an unreadable
     one), so exit 0 with empty stdout becomes an unrecognised answer and reads
     NOT-RUN;
   - **a required non-empty token on an empty answer** — the command prints
     something meaning "none", so silence is never an answer at all;
   - **a self-describing handshake**: an extra protocol form the guard calls
     first, which a command that cannot look fails;
   - **a conformance probe the guard ships**, run against any command it is
     pointed at, refusing the override rather than the answer — closest in
     spirit to `tickets/AGENTS.md`'s "before trusting a scan that returns
     nothing, run it against a case known to be positive";
   - **accept the boundary and document it**, if the honest answer is that a
     replaceable command cannot be made to prove anything and the guard should
     say so where a workshop porting it will read it. This is a real option and
     must be weighed, not used as a fallback for not deciding.
2. Apply the decision to `bench/forge_open_prs.py` so the shipped default is
   the worked example of a compliant command, not an exception to the rule.
3. Keep the test stand-ins honest under the new requirement: whatever a
   compliant command must prove, `forge_stub()` proves it and `broken_forge()`
   does not.
4. Do not widen the change into rule 3's semantics. The payload-difference
   condition, the ancestry exemption, the suggestion, and the exit-0-beside-a-
   NOT-RUN-line decision were all reviewed and approved at #533 round 1 and are
   not in scope.

## Test

- **Red first, and it is the seat's own reproduction:** a `SITTER_FORGE_COMMAND`
  that exits 0 and prints nothing. Today the guard answers
  `OK (rule 3): 0 open pull-request head(s) read` and exits 0; the arm must
  require NOT-RUN. Build the stub before the fix and see the green, so the
  fixture is known to discriminate rather than assumed to.
- A command that is genuinely looking at a forge with no open pull requests must
  still be able to report that, and be believed. Whatever mechanism is chosen,
  one arm holds this apart from the arm above — if the two cannot be told apart
  by the fixtures, the fix has moved the conflation rather than closed it.
- The existing rule-3 arms keep their verdicts, the five NOT-RUN arms included.

## Verification

- [ ] `python3 -m pytest tests/test_check_sitter_version.py -q`
- [ ] `python3 bench/check_sitter_version.py --root .` against the live forge,
      which must still read the real open pull requests rather than NOT-RUN on
      its own default command
- [ ] `make check`
- [ ] The seat's stub reproduced green BEFORE the fix and NOT-RUN after, quoted
      as observed

## Invariants

- Rule 3's approved semantics are untouched: the DIFFERENT-payload condition,
  the ancestry exemption that leaves a merged or own-lane head to rule 1, the
  step-up suggestion, and NOT-RUN on its own line beside a green exit.
- The four pre-0779 NOT-RUN cases keep answering NOT-RUN and keep reddening the
  gate.
- No network call is required for the local rules, and `make check` stays
  runnable offline.
- The override stays usable for what it is for: driving the suite offline and
  running a positive control against real forge data. A requirement that can
  only be satisfied by a stub that lies has failed.

## Exit criteria

- A command pointed at by `SITTER_FORGE_COMMAND` that has not looked cannot be
  read as a forge with no open pull requests — and a command that has looked at
  an empty forge can still say so and be believed.
